## Environment

<!--
Variables the running system requires, named IDENTICALLY across code,
`.env.example`, and infra (compose/helm). `doctrina contract check` warns
when a variable here is missing from `.env.example` — the DATABASE_URL /
KAFKA_BROKER drift class.

Values declares an ENUM when the variable is one. Write the alternation
with escaped pipes so the table survives: `none\|critical\|serious`.
check then fails an .env.example value outside the set (RT04), and warns
when the consumer never mentions any member — an enum nothing validates.
Leave Values empty or `—` for free-form variables.
-->

| Variable     | Required | Values | Example                                   |
|--------------|----------|--------|-------------------------------------------|
| DATABASE_URL | yes      | —      | postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/app   |

## Wiring

<!--
Where each variable's value COMES FROM, and what reads it. This is the
table that closes the gap between "I configured it" and "the process saw
it": a variable can be set in the CI provider and still never reach the
program, because no `env:` line ever exports it.

  Origin    vars | secrets | literal | local
            vars and secrets are CI-injected, and CI injects the EMPTY
            STRING for one that is unset — not "absent". That is why a
            `getenv(NAME, default)` default silently never applies.
  Workflow  repo-relative path to the workflow/pipeline file.
  Job/Step  the job (or step) whose env: block must export it.
  Consumer  the file that reads it.

`doctrina contract check` then verifies (RT01/RT02) that the workflow
really exports it, from the origin and under the name declared, and
(RT03) that the consumer does not give it a default only an ABSENT
value would trigger.

Delete the rows that do not apply. A contract with no Wiring rows is
not "verified" — it is unchecked, and check says so.
-->

| Variable | Origin | Workflow | Job/Step | Consumer |
|----------|--------|----------|----------|----------|
| <NAME>   | vars   | <path>   | <job>    | <file>   |

## Selectors

<!--
The test selectors (BDD tags, suite names, filter expressions) this
project DISPATCHES on. A selector that matches nothing runs zero cases
and still exits 0 — a green job that tested nothing.

  Source   glob for the files the selectors live in.
  Pattern  a regex whose FIRST GROUP captures the selector name.
  Used by  the selectors a pipeline actually dispatches on.

Doctrina knows no test runner: it extracts with the pattern you declare
and checks each selector in "Used by" against what it found (RT05),
naming a near-miss when only the separator differs.

Example (Behave): features/**/*.feature with pattern @([a-z0-9_-]+)
-->

| Selector | Source | Pattern | Used by |
|----------|--------|---------|---------|
| <NAME>   | <glob> | <regex> | <tag>   |

